Hi Jun,

Thank you for your feedback.

> 41. In the section "2PC transaction case with two epoch overflows", some
of the PrevProducerId are still set to -1.

I went through the example again and I think the values of PrevProducerId
are compliant with KIP-890; according to KIP-890, PrevProducerId is set
only when the transaction is transitioned into the PrepareCommit state and
then it's preserved in CompleteCommit state (or PrepareAbort/CompleteAbort
correspondingly), but then it can be reset to -1 when InitProducerId is
processed.  So

- InitProducerId: PrevProducerId=3D-1
- AddPartitionsToTxn: PrevProducerId=3D-1
- (a bunch of InitProducerId(true)): PrevProducerId=3D-1
- Commit: PrevProducerId=3DN
- (complete commit): PrevProducerId=3DN

> 45. Now that KIP-1022 is accepted, could we add a section on upgrade
using the transaction.version feature?

The reason KIP-890 needs a transaction.version feature is because it's a
protocol change and needs to be visible on the client (there is workflow
that client needs to execute to transparently upgrade or downgrade
transaction protocol).  KIP-939 is a new feature, so it doesn't need an
upgrade workflow on the client and we already have a config to turn it on
or off on the brokers.

The persistent data format is downgradable (to KIP-915) and I updated the
proposal to use TransactionTimeoutMs=3DMAX_INT as a flag (previous proposal
was to use -1) to indicate that this is a 2PC transaction, this way, if we
downgrade, 2PC transactions would still be treated as transaction with long
timeout even by old brokers.

I also added some explanation in the Compatibility, Deprecation, and
Migration Plan section, that includes a proposal to tweak KIP-890
implementation to respect TransactionTimeoutMs=3DMAX_INT as infinite timeou=
t.

-Artem


