d=20
> what I would recommend in each:
>
> Case A: Open source accelerators for supported compression codecs (e.g=
.=20
> zstd) 1. Try to add your accelerator to an existing upstream=20
> implementation (e.g. zstd-jni), so that whenever that library is used,=20
> people benefit from your accelerator.
> 2. Fork an existing implementation, and propose that the Kafka project=20
> use your fork.
>
> Case B: Closed-source accelerators for supported compression codecs=20
> (e.g. zstd) 1. Fork an existing implementation, and structure your for=
k=20
> such that it can be swapped out at runtime by operators that want a=20
> particular accelerator.
> 2. Kafka can add a Java pluggable interface to the broker and clients=20
> to pick among the accelerated and non-accelerated plugins, falling bac=
k=20
> to non-accelerated "reference implementations" as necessary. This=20
> wouldn't require protocol changes.
>
> Case C: Accelerators for unsupported open source compression codecs=20
> (e.g. brotli) 1. I think that these should be proposed as official=20
> codecs for Kafka to support, and then the acceleration can be=20
> implemented as in Case A or B.
>
> Case D: Accelerators for unsupported closed-source compression codecs=20
> These are codecs that would require a fully pluggable implementation,=20
> and reserved bits in the binary protocol. They are also the codecs=20
> which are most damaging to the ecosystem. If you have a specific=20
> proprietary codec in mind please say so, otherwise I want to invoke th=
e=20
> YAGNI principle here.
>
> Thanks,
> Greg
