> 
> Imagine the user’s application is writing information to a database instead of Kafka. If there’s a table with a CHAR(1) column and this SQL statement was attempted, what should happen?
> 
>      INSERT INTO foo VALUES (’not sure’);
> 
> Yes, that DML would fail, sure, but would the user expect that the connection used by database library would get stuck in some kind of error state? A user would be able catch the error and either continue or abort, based on their business rules.
> 
> So I agree with what I believe you’re implying: we shouldn’t poison the Producer/TransactionManager on certain types of application-level errors in send().
> 
> Kirk
